          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found says reliever-only treatment is no longer appropriate for asthma at any severity. It says every adult and adolescent with asthma should receive a regular inhaled-corticosteroid-containing treatment, because a short-acting reliever alone leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is linked to severe flare-ups. It also says that using several reliever inhalers a year is itself a sign of higher risk. This comes from the asthma guideline, \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy.\"\n\nI could not confirm whether bodies disagree on this point. The asthma guideline is marked as having a section on where guidance differs, but the part I received did not include it, so I can't tell you what that section says. The results also did not cover other guidance bodies' positions.\n\nIf you're asking about your own treatment, please discuss it with the clinician who manages your asthma. Don't change your inhalers based on this summary.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
